196
00:08:53,040 --> 00:08:57,440
Okay, the next step, number five, is to determine your point of view character.

197
00:08:58,340 --> 00:08:59,360
From whose perspective?

198
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:11,740
You tell your story can be complicated because point of view involves more than simply deciding what voice to use between first person, I, me, second person, you, your, or third person, he, she, or it.

199
00:09:11,740 --> 00:09:17,900
It also involves deciding who will, in essence, serve as your story's camera as your point of view character.

200
00:09:18,760 --> 00:09:22,640
The cardinal rule is one perspective character per scene.

201
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:26,780
But I prefer only one per chapter and ideally one per novel.

202
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:29,340
Readers experience everything in your story.

203
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:31,300
You can tell your story from this character's perspective.

204
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:39,660
What your point of view character sees, hears, touches, smells, tastes, and thinks is all you can convey.

205
00:09:39,960 --> 00:09:42,800
You're in his mind, employing his senses and emotions.

206
00:09:43,660 --> 00:09:48,480
Now, many beginning writers mistakenly assume this limits them to a first person voice, but it doesn't.

207
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:54,480
In fact, most novels are written in the third person, limited to that one perspective character, your lead.

208
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:59,160
That means the story is limited to one perspective character at a time.

209
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:03,600
And that character ought to be the one with the most at stake in each scene.

210
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:11,600
Now, it's true that writing in the first person makes it easier and sound more natural to limit yourself to that one perspective character.

211
00:10:11,600 --> 00:10:15,600
But third person limited is most popular for a reason.

212
00:10:15,600 --> 00:10:21,600
While an omniscient viewpoint would allow hopping in and out of the heads of several characters in each scene,

213
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:27,600
this style, so popular a hundred years and more ago, has become archaic and extremely hard to sell.

214
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:29,080
Yet many begin to understand the concept of the first person voice.

215
00:10:29,080 --> 00:10:35,320
Many beginning writers who grew up on the classics mistakenly write their first novel from a godlike omniscient viewpoint

216
00:10:35,320 --> 00:10:38,320
and then wonder why publishers aren't interested.

217
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:40,320
That's an error you can't afford to make.

218
00:10:41,320 --> 00:10:47,320
Now, don't assume that first person or third person limited makes it impossible to reveal your orbital characters.

219
00:10:47,320 --> 00:10:54,320
Study contemporary fiction to see how successful authors accomplish this through the perspectives of their main characters.

