Wow, I'm reading on, aren't you?

241
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:22,080
Third is the philosophical.

242
00:12:22,080 --> 00:12:28,080
And one of the most well-known philosophical opening lines comes from Leo Tolstoy's classic Anna Karenina.

243
00:12:28,080 --> 00:12:29,080
It reads,

244
00:12:29,080 --> 00:12:31,080
Happy families are all alike.

245
00:12:31,080 --> 00:12:35,080
Every unhappy family is unhappy in its own way.

246
00:12:35,080 --> 00:12:37,080
And finally, poetic.

247
00:12:37,080 --> 00:12:43,080
This example of such an opener comes from James Crumley's The Last Good Kiss.

248
00:12:43,080 --> 00:12:51,080
When I finally caught up with Abraham Traherne, he was drinking beer with an alcoholic bulldog named Fireball Roberts

249
00:12:51,080 --> 00:12:58,080
in a ramshackle joint just outside of Sonoma, California, drinking to heart right out of a fine spring afternoon.

250
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:02,080
Regardless which type of opening line you choose, don't stop there.

251
00:13:02,080 --> 00:13:10,080
Many novels begin strong and immediately fizzle as the author slips back into explaining, describing, scene setting, etc.

252
00:13:10,080 --> 00:13:14,080
Once you have the reader by the throat, hold on till the end.

253
00:13:14,080 --> 00:13:18,080
All right, step number seven, trigger the theater of your reader's mind.

254
00:13:18,080 --> 00:13:24,080
Have you ever noticed that the movie version of your favorite novel rarely lives up to the book itself?

255
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:28,080
How often have you heard or said yourself, I like the book better?

256
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:30,080
I think the reason is obvious.

257
00:13:30,080 --> 00:13:39,080
Even with all its high-tech computer-generated imagery, Hollywood can't compete with the imaginative and dramatic theater of the reader's mind.

258
00:13:39,080 --> 00:13:46,080
Our job as writers is not to force readers to see things as we see them, but rather to trigger their own imaginations.

259
00:13:46,080 --> 00:13:50,080
Suggest just enough to engage their minds.

260
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:52,080
That's where the magic happens.

261
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:57,080
Give the reader a role in the experience so he can deduce and see what's going on

262
00:13:57,080 --> 00:13:58,080
without spilling it.

263
00:13:58,080 --> 00:14:00,080
You can't just go on feeding him every detail.

264
00:14:00,080 --> 00:14:04,080
The key to this is showing rather than telling.

265
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:09,080
You've likely heard this before, but don't despair if it confuses you.

266
00:14:09,080 --> 00:14:13,080
Here's the difference between telling and showing.

267
00:14:13,080 --> 00:14:22,080
Telling simply informs your reader rather than allowing him a role in the experience by deducing what you want him to know by what you've shown.

268
00:14:22,080 --> 00:14:27,080
You're telling, not showing, when you simply report that a character is tall or angry or cold.

269
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:37,080
If your character is tall, your goal should be to show others looking up when they talk to him, or have him ducked to get through a door.

270
00:14:37,080 --> 00:14:41,080
That way the reader deduces that he's tall without your having to tell him.

271
00:14:41,080 --> 00:14:50,080
Rather than saying your character is angry, show his face flush, his throat tighten, his voice rise, or show him slam his fist on the table.

272
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:53,080
When you show, you don't have to tell.

273
00:14:53,080 --> 00:14:56,080
Rather, you engage the theater of your reader's mind and invite him to show.

274
00:14:56,080 --> 00:15:05,080
Okay, step number eight in how to write a novel is to ramp up your main character's trouble.

275
00:15:05,080 --> 00:15:10,080
You've grabbed your reader with a riveting opener and plunged your hero into terrible trouble.

276
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:18,080
Now, make sure everything he does to get out of that trouble only serves to make things progressively worse.

277
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:24,080
Too many amateurs make their hero's life too easy, the way we want our own real lives to be.

278
00:15:24,080 --> 00:15:26,080
Say their main character is a private eye.

279
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:39,080
They give him great looks, good health, a nice car, the latest, most powerful handgun, a happy, gorgeous spouse, a beautiful apartment, a plush office, and a rich client with a juicy case to solve.

280
00:15:39,080 --> 00:15:44,080
Now, unless you're spoofing the genre, that's going to make for one boring story.

281
00:15:44,080 --> 00:15:52,080
When you run into a brick wall and your story lies flat after 20 or 30 pages, you'll discover you've made things too easy for your lead character.

282
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:55,080
If you take nothing else from this video, remember this.

283
00:15:56,080 --> 00:15:59,080
Conflict is the engine of fiction.

284
00:15:59,080 --> 00:16:05,080
Rather than setting your hero up for success by supplying him everything he needs, try the opposite.

285
00:16:05,080 --> 00:16:07,080
Try pulling everything out from under him.

286
00:16:07,080 --> 00:16:11,080
Give him an injury that mars his face, a disease that plagues him.

287
00:16:11,080 --> 00:16:20,080
Have his car stolen, his weapon go missing, his wife leave him, his business dry up so he can't pay his rent, his office burned down.

288
00:16:20,080 --> 00:16:23,080
And that client who shows up with the juicy case?

289
00:16:23,080 --> 00:16:24,080
She's destitute.

290
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:25,080
What's he going to do now?

291
00:16:25,080 --> 00:16:28,080
All of a sudden, you have a real story.

292
00:16:28,080 --> 00:16:32,080
Conflict keeps your reader on the edge of his seat.

293
00:16:32,080 --> 00:16:39,080
The next step, number nine in how to write a novel, is to make your hero's predicament appear hopeless.

294
00:16:39,080 --> 00:16:45,080
Writing coach and novelist Angela Hunt refers to this crucial plot point as the bleakest moment.

295
00:16:45,080 --> 00:16:48,080
It's where even you wonder how you're going to write your way out of it.

296
00:16:48,080 --> 00:16:54,080
Here's where the once reprobate lover who has become a changed man and a devoted fiance,

297
00:16:54,080 --> 00:16:57,080
falls off the wagon the night before the wedding.

298
00:16:57,080 --> 00:17:01,080
Caught red-handed doing drugs and drinking and cavorting with another woman,

299
00:17:01,080 --> 00:17:06,080
he sees his true love storm off, vowing to never speak to him again.

300
00:17:06,080 --> 00:17:11,080
Imagine the nadir, the low point, the bleakest moment for your lead character.

301
00:17:11,080 --> 00:17:17,080
Your ability to make things look irreparable can make or break you as a novelist.

302
00:17:17,080 --> 00:17:19,080
This is not easy, believe me.

303
00:17:19,080 --> 00:17:23,080
You'll be tempted to give your protagonist a break, invent an escape,

304
00:17:23,080 --> 00:17:25,080
or inject a miracle.

305
00:17:25,080 --> 00:17:27,080
Don't you dare.

306
00:17:27,080 --> 00:17:30,080
The bleakest moment forces your hero to take action,

307
00:17:30,080 --> 00:17:35,080
to use every new muscle and skill developed from facing a book full of obstacles,

308
00:17:35,080 --> 00:17:39,080
and prove that things only appeared beyond repair.

309
00:17:39,080 --> 00:17:45,080
The more hopeless you can render the situation, the more powerful your ending will be.

310
00:17:45,080 --> 00:17:49,080
Step ten, then, is to bring it all to a head.

311
00:17:49,080 --> 00:17:52,080
The ultimate resolution, the peak emotional point of your story,

312
00:17:52,080 --> 00:17:55,080
comes when your hero faces his final test.

313
00:17:55,080 --> 00:18:01,080
The stakes must appear insurmountable and failure an irreversible option.

314
00:18:01,080 --> 00:18:06,080
The conflict building throughout now crescendos to an ultimate confrontation

315
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:09,080
and all the major book-length setups are paid off.

316
00:18:09,080 --> 00:18:12,080
Give readers the payoff for which they've been set up.

317
00:18:12,080 --> 00:18:17,080
Reward their sticking with you and let the fireworks play out in full.

318
00:18:17,080 --> 00:18:20,080
But remember, the climax is not the end.

319
00:18:20,080 --> 00:18:21,080
That's still to come.

320
00:18:21,080 --> 00:18:26,080
In step eleven, where you leave your reader wholly satisfied.

321
00:18:26,080 --> 00:18:30,080
Because climaxes are so dramatic, endings often just peter out.

322
00:18:30,080 --> 00:18:32,080
Don't let that happen.

323
00:18:32,080 --> 00:18:34,080
Great endings have two things in common.

324
00:18:34,080 --> 00:18:38,080
They put the reader first, honoring his investment of time and money,

325
00:18:38,080 --> 00:18:43,080
and they keep the hero the hero, on stage till the last word.

326
00:18:43,080 --> 00:18:46,080
Now, the ending won't be as dramatic or action-filled as the climax,

327
00:18:46,080 --> 00:18:48,080
but it must be every bit as riveting.

328
00:18:48,080 --> 00:18:50,080
It should tie up loose ends, sure,

329
00:18:50,080 --> 00:18:53,080
but it also needs to pack an emotional wallop.

330
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:55,080
Don't rush it.

331
00:18:55,080 --> 00:18:59,080
Take your time and write a fully satisfying ending

332
00:18:59,080 --> 00:19:02,080
that drops the curtain with a resounding thud.

333
00:19:02,080 --> 00:19:06,080
All right, it's time for your bonus step and your free download.

334
00:19:06,080 --> 00:19:11,080
Bonus step twelve for how to write a novel is to separate your writing and editing.

335
00:19:11,080 --> 00:19:15,080
I recommend editing and revising as part of your daily process

336
00:19:15,080 --> 00:19:19,080
so you don't write an entire draft before you decide what might need adjusting.

337
00:19:19,080 --> 00:19:23,080
But it's also crucial you keep these two elements entirely separate.

338
00:19:23,080 --> 00:19:26,080
Otherwise, your work may slow to a crawl.

339
00:19:26,080 --> 00:19:30,080
When I'm writing my first draft, I take off my perfectionist cap

340
00:19:30,080 --> 00:19:32,080
and tell my inner critic to shut up.

341
00:19:32,080 --> 00:19:38,080
I get the story down, not stopping to worry about cliches, redundancies, or even lacks of logic.

342
00:19:38,080 --> 00:19:42,080
I tell myself that the next morning I can let my perfectionism take over

343
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:44,080
and rewrite to my heart's content.

344
00:19:44,080 --> 00:19:48,080
My goal is to be happy with every word so I become a ferocious self-proclaimed critic.

345
00:19:49,080 --> 00:19:51,080
I'm not a self-editor.

346
00:19:51,080 --> 00:19:56,080
While revising, I always follow this 21-part checklist and fine-tune every word,

347
00:19:56,080 --> 00:20:00,080
and it's yours free to download and use as you revise your own work.

348
00:20:00,080 --> 00:20:03,080
Simply click the link on the screen.

349
00:20:03,080 --> 00:20:06,080
I also put the link in the description of this video.

350
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:10,080
All right, that wraps up this training on how to write a novel.

351
00:20:10,080 --> 00:20:15,080
If you found it helpful, please like it, comment on it, and share it with others.

352
00:20:15,080 --> 00:20:18,080
You can also subscribe to my channel to get more in-depth writer training.

353
